# Flutter UI Structure

## One class per screen/widget file

- Each screen or widget file should contain **one public class**.
- Do **not** add extra private widget classes in the same file (e.g. `_HeroImage`, `_BottomCta`, `_HeaderSection`).

## Split UI inside a single class

- **Small UI parts** → private builder methods on the same class:
  - `_buildHeroImage()`
  - `_buildBottomCta()`
- **Large or reusable UI** → separate widget file under `widgets/`:
  - `onboarding_feature_item.dart` → `OnboardingFeatureItem`

## Examples

```dart
// ❌ BAD — extra private widget classes in the same file
class OnboardingScreen extends StatelessWidget { ... }
class _HeroImage extends StatelessWidget { ... }
class _BottomCta extends StatelessWidget { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — one class, small sections as methods
class OnboardingScreen extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Column(
      children: [
        _buildHeroImage(),
        _buildBottomCta(),
      ],
    );
  }

  Widget _buildHeroImage() { ... }
  Widget _buildBottomCta() { ... }
}

// ✅ GOOD — large/reusable piece in its own file
// widgets/onboarding_feature_item.dart
class OnboardingFeatureItem extends StatelessWidget { ... }
```

## When to extract a new file

- UI is reused in multiple screens
- Widget logic is large enough to hurt readability in one file
- It represents a clear standalone component (card, list item, form section)

Do not extract just to avoid a private `_build...()` method.

